__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.log
runs/
*.db
*.sqlite

# Operational handover material stays local. It names live processes, pids, panes, a
# schedule and this machine, and the repository is public: see docs/PUBLIC-SAFETY.md
# and tools/public_safety.py, which enforces the same list in content and in names.
NIGHT-*
night-snapshot-*/
night-snapshot-*
snapshot-*/

# The morning page is written for the operator and dated for the day it was written: it
# restates the state of the machine at that hour and is stale by the next one. It stays
# on disk as a working document and off the repo, because it is a snapshot of the run,
# not research.
docs/morning.html

# Credentials and key material never belong in a public repository.
.env
*.env
*.pem
*.key
*_deploy_*
id_rsa*
id_dsa*
id_ecdsa*
id_ed25519*

# Scratch space: work in progress, patches and logs that are not evidence.
tmp/
scratch/

# Solana key material (added by SOLANA-INSTALL-AND-TEST). A keypair file is the
# secret itself: 64 integers in JSON. Kept out of the repo by name here, and by
# shape in .public-denylist, which is the check that actually runs before a commit.
authority*.json
*keypair*.json
**/authority*.json
**/*keypair*.json

# Rust build products for the on-chain program (added by ONCHAIN-WIRE). `target/` holds the
# .so and `cap_hook-keypair.json`, which is the secret that owns the program id: neither is
# ever committed. The keypair is refused again by shape in .public-denylist.
programs/cap_hook/target/
**/target/deploy/

# 64 KB of dependency pins that `cargo-build-sbf` regenerates on every machine that builds the
# program. Kept on disk for a reproducible local build, out of the tree because it is a
# generated artefact of the toolchain, not source.
programs/cap_hook/Cargo.lock

# A rehearsal, not evidence (added by ONCHAIN-WIRE). `tools/devnet_publish.py --local` writes
# this and the console refuses to render it as devnet; keeping it out of the tree means it
# cannot be mistaken for the real artefact by a reader who does not run the code.
records/devnet-artefacts.local.json

# The v2 local proof (added by ONCHAIN-V2). tools/publish_v2.py deploys a NEW program under a
# NEW keypair to a solana-test-validator this machine starts and kills, and writes what it
# proved here: a local rehearsal, not evidence of anything on a public cluster, and not to be
# mistaken for records/devnet-artefacts.json, which is the frozen devnet record.
records/onchain-v2.local.json

# The resume file (added by DEVNET-RESUME). While a run is half finished this is the only
# place the mint, the token accounts and the signatures exist, and the next run reuses all of
# them instead of minting a second mint. It is a run's bookkeeping on this machine, not
# evidence: the artefact is the record that gets published, and this is what made it
# possible to finish it. tools/devnet_publish.py also refuses to write a path or a key handle
# into it, so a copy that escapes is a copy that leaks nothing.
records/devnet-progress.json
records/devnet-progress.local.json

# The trading-swarm harness (added by MARKET-SWARM). A run of it produces three things that
# must never be committed: the synthetic ledger datasets, the throwaway keypairs that sign
# the trades, and the validator's own ledger directory. "The repo is code only" applies.
.swarm-runs/

# Working notes taken while the harness was designed, including the reuse map of the existing
# scripts and the forge runtime's wire protocol. Facts from them are folded into
# records/findings/ with anything machine-specific removed; the raw notes stay local.
market_swarm/.research/

# The video run's own working papers (added by VIDEO-READINESS). Choreography, rehearsal
# notes, reviews and delivery notes name live processes, panes, a schedule and this machine,
# which is handover material and not research: docs/PUBLIC-SAFETY.md forbids publishing it.
# They stay on disk and one `git add -A` cannot reach them. Nothing in the research record
# cites them, and a film frame is not evidence about the product either.
records/video/

# The region gate is parked (added by REGION-CAPS release prep). It was built as a Rust
# program and compiled once on this machine; the build products are 600 MB of toolchain
# output and the pin file is generated by every machine that builds it, so neither is
# source. The program itself stays in the tree as the design record; the cap the engine
# enforces comes from the off-chain rules table, not from this crate.
programs/region_gate/target/
programs/region_gate/Cargo.lock
